      "narrative": "The copper coin stills between her fingers. For a long breath nothing moves \u2014 not her hands, not her face, not the dust motes hanging in the grey light.\n\nThen she sighs, and the tension breaks.\n\n\"Old Bram,\" she says, the name dropping into the quiet like a stone into still water. \"Bram ran the passage. Had the only key that worked. He'd take folk through for a fee \u2014 never asked questions, never kept a ledger. When they found him face-down at the landing, the key was gone. The gate's been dead ever since.\"\n\nShe watches you now, measuring your reaction. \"That's who you're looking for. But he's six days cold, and whatever he knew went into the mud with him.\""
    },
