      "narrative": "The door of the Copper Finch exhales a breath of warm, ale-thick air as you push it open. The morning hits you \u2014 damp and cool, carrying the smell of wet stone and distant woodsmoke. Behind you, the tavern's copper lamps gutter in the draft. Ahead, the street curves between leaning timber buildings, their upper stories jutting toward one another like old conspirators.\n\nThe Ash Gate rises at the street's end.\n\nIt takes you three minutes to reach it. The cobbles grow rougher as you approach, weeds pushing through the gaps, and the buildings on either side fall away into empty lots \u2014 foundations collapsed, roofs caved in, none rebuilt. As if the gate radiates a quiet blight that discourages habitation.\n\nUp close, the Ash Gate is a cracked black arch maybe twelve feet high, set into a wall that stretches off into ivy-choked ruin on either side. The stone is dark, almost vitreous in places, as though it once endured tremendous heat. The gate itself \u2014 a double door of blackened iron banded with rust \u2014 stands shut. A heavy lock dangles from a hasp on the right-hand door, its mechanism crusted with oxidation.\n\nNo keyhole is visible. The lock appears to have been sealed from within, or perhaps fused shut by age.\n\nAt the base of the gate, a narrow stone landing overlooks a channel of dark, slow-moving water \u2014 the ferryman's dock. The water laps against moss-slicked stones. A single frayed rope hangs from a cleat, trailing into the current.\n\nNo body remains. No bloodstains. Just the silence of a place that has been still for a very long time."
    },
