      "narrative": "The walk back is quicker. The morning has brightened a shade \u2014 pale sun straining through the overcast \u2014 and the streets feel less hollow now that you're moving away from the gate. The leaning timber buildings resume their watch as you pass, and the smell of woodsmoke sharpens as you approach the tavern.\n\nThe door of the Copper Finch groans open.\n\nInside, the air is warm and stale, heavy with the ghosts of last night's ale and tallow. Copper lamps hang dark along the walls. The place is near empty at this hour \u2014 a single figure hunched over a far table, nursing something steaming. The barman wipes a glass behind the counter, glances up, then back down.\n\nYour old table sits where you left it. Scarred oak, a notch in the near corner where someone put a knife through years ago, a faint ring of old wine staining the grain. The chair scrapes as you pull it out and sit.\n\nThe barman doesn't approach. The figure at the far table doesn't look up. The tavern settles around you like a held breath, waiting for you to break its quiet."
